# PROVENANCE: measured, not generated. Every response byte below was captured
# from a genuine DESFire EV3 4K over PC/SC by a PC/SC capture tool
# and is used here as an expectation for our emulator. As with the other
# hardware_physical_*.vec files, this vector is the opposite of the rest
# of the corpus: the others were recorded from the reference engine, so they
# pin what we decided; this one pins what the silicon does. A mismatch here is
# either a real divergence or a difference we can justify in writing, never a
# vector to be re-recorded from our engine.
#
# Card: UID 048222F2CF1890, hardware 51.0, software 3.0, storage 4K. Formatted
# with FormatPICC under the all-zero PICC test key, then provisioned with
# a synthetic structure whose every key is a published test key. That is what
# makes an authenticated capture safe to commit and replayable: no key material
# enters the tree, and our engine derives the same session key from the same
# pinned nonces.
#
# RndA is the fixed constant the recorder uses; RndB is the card's, recovered
# by decrypting its first authentication frame with the test key and
# pinned here with inject-random so our engine offers the same challenge.
# What this vector is for: authenticated ReadData and WriteData under an
# AES session in all three communication modes - plain, MACed and
# enciphered - measured against silicon. Our secure messaging had until
# now been checked only against our own implementation, so this is its
# first external check. The recorder verifies each write by reading the
# same octets back through the same mode, so a byte here belongs to an
# exchange the card and the recorder agreed on.

name: genuine EV3 4K, AES session read and write in every comm mode
case-id: hardware-physical-ev3-4k-aes-comm-modes
origin: physical-card
source: captured hardware trace
requires: auth-aes
uid: 048222F2CF1890
picc-key-settings-1: 0F
picc-key-settings-2: 01
picc-auth-mode: D40
picc-key-len: 16
picc-generation: EV3
picc-storage: 4096

scan
# The application. KS1 0F leaves create and delete of files free, so
# the structure below is built without an application authentication;
# whether silicon really honours that bit is what the free-create
# vector measures separately.
status   90CA000005B3B2B10F8400 => 00
status   905A000003B3B2B100 => 00
#
# Three Standard Data files of 32 octets, one per communication mode:
# 00 plain, 01 MACed, 03 enciphered. Access rights are logical 0x1111
# throughout - read, write, read&write and change all name key 1 - so
# the only variable between the three is the communication mode.
status   90CD0000070100111120000000 => 00
status   90CD0000070201111120000000 => 00
status   90CD0000070303111120000000 => 00
#
# GetFileSettings for each, unauthenticated, so the communication mode
# the card believes it stored is on the record before anything is
# written through it.
exchange native-apdu 90F50000010100 => 000011112000009100
exchange native-apdu 90F50000010200 => 000111112000009100
exchange native-apdu 90F50000010300 => 000311112000009100
#
# Authenticate as key 1, the key every access right above names.
inject-random B803EAD916AF3EBB268BD9B9A7539864
exchange native-apdu 90AA0000010100 => 849380F7E8355A9B4D1A1204E23134A991AF
exchange native-apdu 90AF000020A446368C81E0136837A975A462888972DE398421A464A593B71FFF9FE2C924C900 => 356E21C69CE26784CA26D539941A3E9C9100
#
# File 01, plain. Write 16 octets, then read them back.
exchange native-apdu 903D00001701000000100000101112131415161718191A1B1C1D1E1F00 => F57E8CB358C566819100
exchange native-apdu 90BD0000070100000010000000 => 101112131415161718191A1B1C1D1E1F8AEF5AC189151C4F9100
#
# File 02, MACed. Write 16 octets, then read them back.
exchange native-apdu 903D00001F02000000100000202122232425262728292A2B2C2D2E2F1F4907D507CB2C8400 => FEE949323084BBB99100
exchange native-apdu 90BD0000070200000010000000 => 202122232425262728292A2B2C2D2E2F04BBD731E27A59AA9100
#
# How the card MACed the answers, measured rather than assumed:
#   file 01 (plain): the write answer carried 8 octet(s) of MAC and it verified, the read answer 8.
#   file 02 (MACed): the write answer carried 8 octet(s) of MAC and it verified, the read answer 8.
#
# File 03, enciphered. Before the round trip, which octets the
# CRC32 inside the cryptogram covers, put to the card as two
# candidate cryptograms over the same 16 octets of data.
#
# First candidate: CRC32 over Cmd||Data, which is what our engine
# computes and verifies.
exchange native-apdu 903D00002703000000100000C3D5BE87397BA73721CCCEB17AC0E06AAD86345918C7A361E3B9CB04A48DD87200 => 911E
#
# A refused cryptogram ends the session, so authenticate again
# before offering the second candidate.
status   905A000003B3B2B100 => 00
inject-random 49B71D69658A0BC2C9D96BA85DC47913
exchange native-apdu 90AA0000010100 => EF3EBFA14C631E08283BA9E8AC1921C691AF
exchange native-apdu 90AF000020D7330DFC590B69518ECE4E62D43BAC2806920481FFCE22E3051B9BCAC2C1978900 => C1CF854D5A3DEBC316B29C4A2F609C4C9100
#
# Second candidate: CRC32 over Cmd||FileNo||Offset||Length||Data,
# the whole command as it appears on the wire.
exchange native-apdu 903D00002703000000100000CF2D717D05480631C630950D0A9CF92752B8986523D2BAB0AB1174549E66BAE000 => F73DE01D23E9A05D9100
exchange native-apdu 90BD0000070300000010000000 => 7960D07E100BA8C9A87822013BB3257E746765494B54197CAA22AB742C53CB039100
